---

The site layer delivers the structure of your site as data, and these components render it. One headless frame, two ready-made shells and a handful of smaller components turn a `Site` into a page that looks like documentation, and you can override or replace each piece.

Which one to start from depends on how much of the layout you want to own. `DocsShell` gives you a working docs layout in one import, and `BlogShell` does the same for a blog. `Frame` keeps the composition but lets you bring your own header and sidebar, and the individual components (`Doc`, `Sidebar`, `Pager`) are there when you compose the page entirely yourself.

## One frame, two shells

**`Frame`** is the headless composition of a sidebar and a main area, with every region overridable: pass a component to _replace_ a region, `false`/`null` to _remove_ it, or nothing to keep the default. It imports no CSS and makes no design decisions:

```svelte
<script>
	import { Frame } from '@ogygia/content';
	import { docs } from '$lib/site.server';   // in a csr=false layout, server-only is fine
	import MyHeader from '$lib/MyHeader.svelte';
</script>

<Frame site={docs} header={MyHeader} footer={null}>
	{@render children()}
</Frame>
```

The shells are **compositions of Frame and the public components**, with no privileged code. `DocsShell` follows the VitePress layout: a top header (brand, links, search, theme toggle), a left sidebar, a content column, and a mobile bottom bar with a slide-up sheet. `BlogShell` is the blog layout, with the same header and mobile chrome but no sidebar and an article-width column. Anything they do, your own composition can do too; they exist so that you don't have to build it.

::: code-group

```svelte [DocsShell]
<script>
	import DocsShell from '@ogygia/content/docs-shell';
	import '@ogygia/content/theme.css';   // the design language (tokens + element styles)
	import '@ogygia/content/shell.css';   // shell layout (header + sidebar + grid + mobile chrome)
	import { meta } from '$lib/site.remote';
	let { children } = $props();

	const shellMeta = await meta();
</script>

<DocsShell meta={shellMeta} base="/docs" title="Docs">
	{@render children()}
</DocsShell>
```

```svelte [BlogShell]
<script>
	import { BlogShell } from '@ogygia/content';
	import '@ogygia/content/theme.css';
	import '@ogygia/content/shell.css';
	let { children } = $props();
</script>

<BlogShell base="/blog" title="Example blog" links={[{ text: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }]}>
	{@render children()}
</BlogShell>
```

:::

Both shells accept either `{site}` directly (the simple path, for csr=false layouts) or `{meta}` from the [meta remote](/docs/content/remotes) (the path that keeps the corpus on the server). Each region a shell renders is a **conditional snippet prop**: pass a snippet to replace it, `null` to remove it, or nothing to keep the built-in one. This site removes the stock header (`header={null}`) and brings its own.

## Styling is opt-in

The shells ship **structure and `.og-*` class hooks, and import no CSS**. For the stock look, import the two stylesheets once in your root layout; for your own look, leave them out and style the `.og-*` classes yourself. Either way, you only load the CSS you ask for.

- **`theme.css`** is the _design language_: the token palette (`--og-*`) and the element styles shared by every shell.
- **`shell.css`** is the shells' _layout_: header, sidebar, grid and mobile chrome.

Because they are separate imports, you can keep the tokens and swap the layout, or keep the layout and repaint it with your own tokens. Every rule lives in `@layer ogygia`, so **any unlayered rule you write takes precedence** without specificity battles or `!important`:

```css
/* your skin: unlayered → beats every @layer ogygia rule with plain selectors */
.og-sidebar { border-right: 1px dashed var(--og-line); }
```

Alternative token themes live under `@ogygia/content/themes/*`; swap the import and keep everything else:

```svelte
<script>
	import '@ogygia/content/themes/thalassa.css';   // or alexandria, daidalos, nephele, …
	import '@ogygia/content/shell.css';
</script>
```

## The components

Each of these is a plain component that you can use without a shell:

- **`<Doc {view} />`** lays out one page from a [`docs.page()`](/docs/content/remotes) view: eyebrow, title, the rendered body, the **On this page** rail from `view.headings`, and prev/next. Props turn the breadcrumbs, the pager and the keep-reading list on or off.
- **`<Sidebar />`** renders the nav tree and tracks the active row. It is a [client island](/docs/regions/client-islands), so its marker moves smoothly across SPA navigations.
- **`<Pager />`** renders the prev/next footer from the outline's `prevNext`.
- **`<OnThisPage headings={…} />`** is the scroll-spy rail. `<Doc>` mounts it, and you can use it on its own when you compose your own article.
- **`<Switcher />`** is the version/locale picker on a [dimensioned](/docs/content/dimensions) site. It is a Bits UI dropdown, and therefore an island.
- **`<Search />` / `<SearchPage />`** are the ⌘K palette and the no-JS results page; see [Search](/docs/content/search).
- **`<BlogList {posts} />` / `<BlogPost {view} />`** are the blog components, a dated index and the article layout. Both read the same `PageView` shape as `<Doc>`.
- **`<TabGroup>` / `<Tab>`** are tabbed panels, which `::: code-group` markdown also produces without any imports (the tabs above are made that way).

## Doc, on its own

The article layout needs nothing but a view, and no shell is required:

```svelte
<script>
	import { Doc } from '@ogygia/content';
	import * as docs from '$lib/site.remote';

	const view = await docs.page(slug);
</script>

<Doc {view} keepReading={view.trail?.related} />
```

The body inside `view` is a baked [region](/docs/regions/held-regions) ticket, so every island the source `.svx` imported wakes on the page exactly as it would in an in-pass render. These docs use `<Doc>`: the article you are reading, its on-this-page rail and the pager below it are all this component.

## Overriding markdown elements

The `components` map on `site()` replaces how markdown elements render, using **real component values in app code** rather than import paths in config. The compiled markup routes overridable tags through one slot, which looks the tag up in the map at render time:

```ts
export const docs = site({
	outline: guides,
	components: { img: OptimizedImage, code: FancyCode }
});
```

The built-in default maps `a` to `Link`, so id-form links (`[Guide](routing)`) resolve against the site's address space and stay correct under any mount prefix. A shell provides the map through context, and tier-2 renders can provide it themselves.

## Do / don't

- **Do** start with `DocsShell`, which gives you a working docs site in one import, and move to `Frame` or the individual components only when you need to.
- **Do** import the CSS once in your root layout. The shells import none themselves, which is what lets you bring your own.
- **Don't** pass `site` through every component. A shell sets it on context, and the components inside read it without props.
- **Don't** reimplement the on-this-page rail or the pager. `view.headings` and `view.trail` already carry the data, and `<Doc>` renders both.
